---
import DesignSystemLayout from "../../../layouts/DesignSystemLayout.astro";
---

<DesignSystemLayout title="Parts" wide>
  <article class="parts">
    <section>
      <h2>Part design</h2>
      <p>
        Parts suggest purposeful machinery and a world with history. Their
        artwork and fictional details give them character; gameplay capabilities
        belong to the catalog and game contracts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare SVG masters with the shared rendered artwork on the
        <a href="/lab/ds/engines">Engines</a>
        and
        <a href="/lab/ds/weapons">Weapons</a>
        pages.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Testing-only marking</h2>
      <p>
        “Testing only” is pure fictional prose: an optional aesthetic extra, not
        a required testing state. It has no gameplay, ranked, ruleset or
        sanctioning meaning and creates no fitting penalty or restriction.
      </p>
      <p>
        A designer may include the marking in a part's SVG master. The shared
        artwork carries that decoration through the rendered poses and Hangar
        card. The Service ion on the Engines page is an example.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Parallel brand-colour lines rise right at 45° when the part is nose
          up.
        </li>
        <li>
          The gap is twice the line width; the master sets the width in part
          units.
        </li>
        <li>
          Lines cover the outer half of one side of the body, clipped inside it.
          Rails, mounts and the nozzle stay bare.
        </li>
        <li>
          Flight draws a cached stamp rather than stroking each line per frame.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</DesignSystemLayout>

<style>
.parts {
  max-width: 70ch;
  padding: var(--spacing-xl);
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--spacing-xl);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

h2 {
  font-size: var(--font-size-heading);
}

a {
  color: var(--color-brand);
}
</style>
